Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||05-04-2001||02:22 PM||||Last time I took a vehicle in to have its AC recharged the mechanic told me they take the gas out, and replace it with a gas that has a dye in it. <P>then they run the system for a while to see where the dye leaks out (because it HAS to be leaking somewhere otherwise it wouldnt need to be recharged).<P>then they take the gas with the dye out, replace all the leaking hoses or parts, and put the proper charge of clean gas in.<P> I was looking at $200+ whatever hoses/parts they found bad.<P>I tried to explain to the guy about ozmosis, and that the system has worked fine for 10 years and if it looses 25% of its charge over the next 10 years I dont care.<P>  He wouldnt have nothing to do with that line of reasoning.<P>  personally I think if its just a little low you can pump it back full and be done with it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-04-2001||02:24 PM||||BTW, I drove the vehicle (my old dodge van) for two years after that, without using the air.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||05-04-2001||02:28 PM||||and when I dontated the van to a charity (two years after the mechanic told me it HAS to be leaking) it still had enough gas in it for the AC to work somewhat.||||reg||1
