Z||000008||Rodrv6||05-04-2001||03:56 PM||||Evacuating the system is done by attatching a vacuum pump and sucking all the gases and air out of the system.  It is needed to remove any air that may have gotten into the system.  If your system is just low on refrigerant, you can just add some more.  If it has been opened to the air or if the refrigerant is totally gone or you are changing from R-12 to R-134 then the system MUST be evacuated before being recharged.  Mixing types of refrigerant with each other or with outside air will cause poor cooling and other problems.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||05-04-2001||04:01 PM||||'evacuating' uses a vacum motor to take out everything including freon, moisture, small particles. then you refill it. you can charge it yourself if its 134, you can even buy it at KMart. If you use the old R12 you have to have a shop do it or find someone (like i did) that has a supply of the R12. My old car had to have a can (pound) added every spring, ac shops used all the gadgets and couldnt find a leak anywhere. just buy a couple of the cans however much your system takes and the little valve that goes over the top of can with a hose to connect to the fitting inside the front trunk (looks like a tire valve) best if you wear glove and safety glasses, freon freezes skin immediatly<BR>just run car with ac on max at lowest temp setting till its all in. I think Fiero takes 3 cans (pounds) ??||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tom88gt||05-05-2001||07:44 PM||||Evacuation only removes moisture from the system and should be done anytime the system is opened.  It is done with a vaccum pump.  You can get the pump from Harbor Freight form about $10.  You will need an air compressor to operate the pump.  I recommend at least a 5 hp, 20 gal compressor.  The vaccum pump consumes a large amount of air.<P>How does it work?  You simply remove one of the valve cores from a service port (high or low).  Attach hose to the port an the vacuum pump and fire up the compressor and let it run for about an hour.  What happens is the atmospheric pressure in the entire AC system is reduced to below the boiling point for water at the abmient tempature.  Just like your blood will boil in outerspace.  Because the is no pressure, water will boil at room tempature.  This will evaporate any moisture in the system.<P>After an hour, remove the pump and replace the valve core.  Your system is no purged of any moisture and ready to charge with freon.  <P>Don't woory about any crap about r-12 and r-134 not being compatible.  Just drain all the old oil you can out of the system.  By a $35 conversion kit (R-134, valve adapters, new oil), evacuate system, fill with new stuff.<P>True, the two different oils do not mix.  But they do not react with each other.  The R-12 oil will not mix with the R-134 freon and any remaining R-12 oil in the system will just "puddle" in the low places and nooks and crannies and sit there.<P>All this is assuming your system is intact and not leaking.  Until you fix the leaks, R-12 or R-134 won't work.<P>I've done 2 cars.  I bought them with non working AC.  I just fixed the leaks.  Evacuated the systems.  Charged with R-134 and "new" oil.  Cold as a witch's twat.||||reg||1
